    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Gnostic groups held that a hidden supreme being stood above a flawed creator, the demiurge, and that salvation came through direct knowledge (gnosis) of one's own divine origin.</li><li>For centuries it was known mainly through the hostile writings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-fathers">Church Fathers</a> such as Irenaeus and Hippolytus, until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nag-hammadi-library">Nag Hammadi library</a> was found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 1945.</li><li>Scholars now debate whether &quot;Gnosticism&quot; is a form of early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, a category created by its opponents or a tradition in its own right, and many question its use as a single label.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nag-hammadi-library">Nag Hammadi library</a>, twelve leather-bound <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">papyrus</a> codices sealed in a jar, was found in 1945 by a local farmer, Muhammed al-Samman, and may have belonged to a nearby Pachomian monastery.</p></aside>
    </section>

