    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 150</span><span class="rf-text">Marcion of Sinope preaches in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, is expelled and starts a congregation that spreads through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 180</span><span class="rf-text">Irenaeus writes Against Heresies (c. 180–185), which names Simon Magus as the inceptor of Gnosticism</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">240</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mani">Mani</a> travels to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greek Kingdom</a> of the Sakas in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> (240–241), before joining the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shapur-i">Shapur I</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">367</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> Athanasius condemns the use of non-canonical books in his Festal Letter, after which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nag-hammadi-library">Nag Hammadi codices</a> may have been buried</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">762</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manichaeism">Manichaeism</a> becomes the state religion of the Uyghur Khaganate</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1209</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albigensian-crusade">Albigensian Crusade</a> (1209–1229) begins; it and conversion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> greatly reduced the remaining Gnostics of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1890</span><span class="rf-text">Jules Doinel re-establishes a Gnostic church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1945</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nag-hammadi-library">Nag Hammadi library</a> is found near Nag Hammadi in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>

