    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/gospel-of-john"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gospel-of-john.jpg" alt="Gospel of John" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/gospel-of-john">Gospel of John</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:JRL19060215_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RylandsImaging, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The origins of Gnosticism are obscure and disputed. Gnostic writings flourished among certain Christian groups in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> during the 2nd century, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> is described as central to its birth. Scholars give several accounts of where it came from. Simone Pétrement and David Brakke argue that it arose as an intra-Christian movement, Pétrement tracing it to tendencies in the letters of Paul and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospel-of-john">Gospel of John</a>. Others, among them Gershom Scholem and Gilles Quispel, hold that it arose within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a>, drawing on merkabah mysticism, and Quispel saw it as an independent Jewish development among Alexandrian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>.</p><p>Earlier scholars proposed Persian origins, as Wilhelm Bousset and Richard Reitzenstein did, but Carsten Colpe showed many of Reitzenstein's hypotheses to be untenable. Others point to Middle Platonism, and a small minority see roots in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, though modern scholarship does not support Buddhist influence on Valentinus or the Nag Hammadi texts. Some scholars use "gnosis" for 1st-century ideas and keep "Gnosticism" for the synthesis of the 2nd century. According to James M. Robinson, no Gnostic text clearly predates <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. The term itself does not appear in ancient sources: Henry More coined it in the 17th century, from the Greek gnostikos that Irenaeus used about 185 for the school of Valentinus.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="beliefs">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Beliefs &amp; Practice</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Gnostic worldviews typically distinguished a hidden, uncorrupted supreme being from an inherently corrupt or flawed demiurge, who was responsible for creating material reality. In the Syrian–Egyptian traditions the supreme Godhead, the Monad, is remote. Lower divine beings, the aeons, emanate from it and together make up the pleroma, the region of light. The last of them, Sophia, gives rise to the demiurge, who is also called Yaldabaoth, Saklas or Samael, and who creates the material world and the archons that preside over it. Divine elements fall into the material realm and are latent in human beings, and redemption comes when they obtain gnosis, esoteric or intuitive knowledge of the divine. Many texts speak not of sin and repentance but of ignorance, illusion and enlightenment.</p><p>Gnostics tended towards asceticism, especially in sexual and dietary practice, and held that internal motivation mattered more than ritual. Views of materiality ranged from inherently evil to merely flawed, and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> from an emanation of the supreme being who descended to bring gnosis to others, to a human mystic who attained enlightenment. Among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandaeism">Mandaeans</a>, Jesus was considered a false messiah. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manichaeism">Manichaeism</a> is called a radical dualism of two coequal forces, against the mitigated dualism of the classic Gnostic movements.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="texts">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Texts &amp; Teachers</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/tertullian"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tertullian.jpg" alt="Tertullian" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/tertullian">Tertullian</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tertullian2.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">André Thevet, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Until 1945 Gnosticism was known mainly from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-fathers">Church Fathers</a> who opposed it, and their writings had an antagonistic bias and were incomplete. Justin Martyr criticised Simon Magus, Menander and Marcion in his First Apology. Irenaeus wrote Against Heresies about 180 to 185. Hippolytus wrote a ten-volume Refutation of all Heresies, of which eight volumes have been found, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tertullian">Tertullian</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> wrote Against the Valentinians about 206. Several of these writers made little effort to record exactly the sects they described.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nag-hammadi-library">Nag Hammadi library</a>, found near Nag Hammadi in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a> in 1945, holds twelve <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">papyrus</a> codices with fifty-two mostly Gnostic treatises, three works from the Corpus Hermeticum and a partial translation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-republic">Plato's Republic</a>. The original language was probably Greek and the codices are in Coptic. A 1st- or 2nd-century date has been proposed for the lost Greek originals, though this is disputed, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> date from the 3rd and 4th centuries. The Apocryphon of John, the oldest text certainly known to be Gnostic, dates from the mid-2nd century. The Gospel of Thomas has many sayings parallel to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">canonical gospels</a>; Helmut Koester holds that its sayings are older, while John P. Meier reports that scholars predominantly conclude that it depends on or parallels the Synoptic Gospels. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandaeism">Mandaeans</a> keep their own scriptures in Mandaic, chiefly the Ginza Rabba.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="spread">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Spread</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Perkins divides the development of Gnosticism into three periods. The mid-2nd to early 3rd century was the high point of the classical Gnostic teachers and their systems, and from the end of the 2nd century to the 4th a reaction by the proto-orthodox church and condemnation as heresy led to decline. The movement spread in areas controlled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> and Arian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a>, and the Persian Empire. Valentinianism spread to Northwest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and east to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. Marcion's congregation, founded after he preached in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> about 150, spread throughout the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, though some scholars do not count him a Gnostic. Basilidianism was almost exclusively limited to Egypt and survived until the end of the 4th century.</p><p>Decline set in during the 3rd century because of the rise of proto-orthodoxy and the deterioration of the Roman Empire. After declining in the western Mediterranean, Gnosticism persisted in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a> until at least the 6th century and remained influential as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> until the late 9th. Ideas then resurfaced in groups such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paulicians">Paulicians</a>, between 650 and 872 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogomilism">Bogomils</a>, who emerged in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> between 927 and 970, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catharism">Cathars</a>. Conversion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albigensian-crusade">Albigensian Crusade</a> (1209–1229) greatly reduced the remaining numbers.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="community">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Community &amp; Society</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mary-magdalene"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mary-magdalene.jpg" alt="Mary Magdalene" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mary-magdalene">Mary Magdalene</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Noli_me_tangere_(Monreale).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sibeaster, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Gnostic groups were diverse and never formed a single church. Modern scholars distinguish a Judean–Israelite branch, including the Elkesaites, a baptismal group active between 100 and 400, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandaeism">Mandaeans</a>; a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a>–Egyptian one, including the Sethians, Valentinians, Basilidians and Serpent Gnostics; and a Persian one, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manichaeism">Manichaeism</a>. Valentinus (c. 100 – c. 180) was a candidate for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> but started his own group when another was chosen, and his school was popular, intellectually vibrant and numerous. Gnostic teachers and early Gnostic sects saw themselves as Christians.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-fathers">Church Fathers</a> denounced Gnostic ideas as heresy, and efforts to destroy Gnostic texts were largely successful, so that very little writing by Gnostic thinkers survives. The Christian heresiologists accused some groups of libertine practice, among them the Cainites, the Carpocratians and the Borborites, but there is little evidence about the nature of the Cainites. Women in most Gnostic literature are portrayed as chaotic and disobedient, while the Nag Hammadi texts place women in roles of leadership and heroism, and some texts, such as the Gospel of Mary, treat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-magdalene">Mary Magdalene</a> as a leader superior to the twelve apostles. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neoplatonism">Neoplatonists</a> such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plotinus">Plotinus</a> also opposed the Gnostics, rejecting their radical dualism.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandaeism">Mandaeism</a> is the only surviving Gnostic religion from antiquity. The Mandaeans are an ethnoreligious group who speak Mandaic, a dialect of Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a>, and live in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and the Iranian province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khuzestan">Khuzestan</a> and in diaspora communities, with 60,000 to 70,000 worldwide. They revere <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-the-baptist">John the Baptist</a> and baptise repeatedly. Yezidism, originally practised in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, may also be regarded as a continuation of Sethianism and Ophitism. Gnostic ideas are also found in Islamic and Kabbalistic thought, though Scholem's claim of Cathar influence on Kabbalah has not been substantiated by extant texts, according to Dan Joseph.</p><p>Since 1890, when Jules Doinel re-established a Gnostic church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, a number of modern Gnostic churches have been founded, among them the Ecclesia Gnostica and the Apostolic Johannite Church, many after the Nag Hammadi discovery. Thinkers such as Schopenhauer, Carl Jung, Hans Jonas and Harold Bloom studied or were influenced by Gnosticism. Since the 1990s scholars have debated whether it is a form of early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, a category created by orthodox Christians, or a distinct tradition. Michael Williams proposed "Biblical-Demiurgic tradition" in 1996, and Karen King argued in 2004 that the term does injustice to the diversity of early Christianity.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Gnosticism is a modern term for diverse religious systems of the 2nd century and after that stressed saving knowledge and set a hidden supreme being above a flawed creator. For centuries it was known mainly through its opponents, until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nag-hammadi-library">Nag Hammadi library</a> was found in 1945. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandaeism">Mandaeism</a> survives as its one ancient continuation.</em></p>

